    81	  floor, and `evaluate()` does not depend on earlier reports, so such a report
    82	  validates whatever precedes it. On any history git can read, the chain from
    83	  the anchor onward is exactly what replaying every report from the root
    84	  gives. A test checks this on random histories, including merged side
    85	  branches with skewed commit dates, against a reference: the v2.12.3 walk
    86	  with its caps removed.
    87	- **Fail-closed is unchanged for everything the floor depends on** (one
    88	  deliberate exception for history before the anchor is described next).
    89	  Only an L3 report anchors. A lower tier's
    90	  validity depends on the segment it governs. A report that would not count
    91	  up close does not count from far away either. With no valid report
    92	  anywhere, the floor still covers everything from the root, the root's own
    93	  files included.
    94	- **One deliberate difference from a full replay:** history *before* the
    95	  anchor is not diffed, so a tree or file there that cannot be read no longer
    96	  makes the floor unknowable (a missing *commit* object still refuses, because
