.coverloop/reports/49268679728fcab83bba6624877cc7a2a7e1bbd9.glm.log:12:## P2 improvements
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:15:Independent diff review. The prior review raised one P1: three callers still collected pathnames line-by-line, so the path-normalisation bypass survived in only_evidence_changed(), changed_files(), and --require-clean-tree. This diff routes all three through _git_paths() (raw NUL parsing) and adds a structural test that fails if any pathname-producing git call sits outside it. Verify the fix is correct and complete, and check nothing broke in those three callers' semantics (None handling, empty results, artifact filtering). Be decisive: if there is no genuine fail-open, say PASS. End with exactly: VERDICT: PASS or VERDICT: ISSUES
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:510:- **Codex CLI** = mandatory independent code/diff reviewer for meaningful PRs.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:563:### Codex CLI — mandatory diff reviewer
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:569:- **Linux sandbox prerequisite.** On Linux, Codex sandboxes via a bundled `bwrap` that needs unprivileged user + network namespaces. On Ubuntu 23.10+/24.04 these are AppArmor-clamped, so Codex's sandbox fails (`uid_map`/`RTM_NEWADDR: Operation not permitted`). Fix the OS at the root (`docs/CODEX_SANDBOX_LINUX.md`) — **never** the `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` flag. Prefer `codex review --uncommitted` (reliable local-diff review; also avoids the `--base` GitHub-MCP "wrong PR" bug). `install.sh` preflights this and prints the remedy.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:579:Invoked via `glm-audit` / `glm-audit-xhigh` (GLM-5.2, `z-ai/glm-5.2`) for cross-layer mismatch at Level 3: app/worker code depending on DB columns/RPCs not deployed everywhere; app auto-deploy vs gated migrations; staging/prod drift; unsafe blanket db push or migration ordering; runbook/STATE/DECISIONS contradictions; route/page assumptions failing on null/missing fields; rollback-order gaps; merged-but-not-live ambiguity. Same hardened text-only CLI, hard secret filter, and `{zdr:true, allow_fallbacks:false, data_collection:deny}` routing as the GLM red-team. Output the 7-column table: `Finding | Severity P0/P1/P2 | Evidence | Why it matters | Required verification | Suggested action | Gate required?`. Every finding is reconciled by Claude; the auditor authorizes nothing.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:590:**Execution:** post-implementation reviewers operate on a FROZEN diff and are INDEPENDENT — run Codex diff review, GLM-POST red-team, and `glm-audit` (+ optional `m3-audit`) CONCURRENTLY, then reconcile in one pass. Only GLM-PRE is serial (it gates the design). Do not re-freeze and re-fan-out unless a P0/P1 fix changes the diff.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:627:**Two tracks.** FULL packet (A–K) by default. **LITE packet** (D, E, F, G/H if applicable, I) is permitted ONLY when ALL hold and are stated explicitly: re-applies a previously red-teamed pattern; no new or altered invariant; no new schema object; no change to deploy/rollback order; no new auth/RLS/money surface — AND you cite the specific prior GLM finding-set reused by its packet/decision id. GLM-PRE may be skipped on the LITE track ONLY when every condition holds AND **the operator confirms LITE eligibility** (not merely the gate); if any condition is uncertain, run FULL. **GLM-POST and the Human Gate remain mandatory on both tracks — never skipped**, with one recorded exception: `--human-gate-scope irreversible` narrows the STOP (never the review, never the tier) to migrations/SQL, money paths and authz/RLS. Choosing it is a deliberate loosening of this rule, not a default. If GLM-PRE returned clean AND the diff faithfully matches the approved design with no new invariant/interface, the post pass is right-sized to Codex diff review + `glm-audit` at HIGH effort; a second xhigh GLM-POST is required only if the diff introduced logic not in the approved design.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:646:**Evidence depth scales with severity.** P0/P1: full record with cited evidence (and the alternate-path check before any false-positive close). P2: one-line disposition (fix-now / backlog / wontfix) — no deep verification, and P2s never block convergence.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:721:A task is CONVERGED and review STOPS when ALL hold: (1) tests/typecheck/lint/build green; (2) zero unresolved P0/P1; (3) every open finding classified per Section 6; (4) the latest reviewer pass produced no NEW P0/P1. Once converged, remaining P2s are logged as follow-ups, NOT fixed-and-re-reviewed in this task. Re-review after a fix is RE-SCOPED to the changed lines only — reviewers may not raise net-new findings OUTSIDE the fix diff (those go to backlog), but a P0/P1 introduced BY the fix itself must be fixed before convergence. Round budget: max 2 GLM rounds and 2 Codex rounds per task; a 3rd round requires the human operator to explicitly authorize the spend. **Repeated REAL findings past the cap = a SIZE signal, not just a spend decision (v2.5):** if each round keeps surfacing genuine (non-cosmetic) P0/P1s, the change is too large to review well — SPLIT the PR into smaller reviewable units rather than grinding a 4th/5th round on one oversized diff (a single PR that needed 5 Codex rounds is the tell). **The round cap NEVER converts an unresolved P0/P1 into a pass — if the cap is reached with any open P0/P1, that is a STOP escalated to the human operator.** `glm-audit`/`m3-audit` are consistency checks, not part of this nitpick loop.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:948:- **Codex CLI** = mandatory independent code/diff reviewer for meaningful PRs.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1001:### Codex CLI — mandatory diff reviewer
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1007:- **Linux sandbox prerequisite.** On Linux, Codex sandboxes via a bundled `bwrap` that needs unprivileged user + network namespaces. On Ubuntu 23.10+/24.04 these are AppArmor-clamped, so Codex's sandbox fails (`uid_map`/`RTM_NEWADDR: Operation not permitted`). Fix the OS at the root (`docs/CODEX_SANDBOX_LINUX.md`) — **never** the `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` flag. Prefer `codex review --uncommitted` (reliable local-diff review; also avoids the `--base` GitHub-MCP "wrong PR" bug). `install.sh` preflights this and prints the remedy.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1017:Invoked via `glm-audit` / `glm-audit-xhigh` (GLM-5.2, `z-ai/glm-5.2`) for cross-layer mismatch at Level 3: app/worker code depending on DB columns/RPCs not deployed everywhere; app auto-deploy vs gated migrations; staging/prod drift; unsafe blanket db push or migration ordering; runbook/STATE/DECISIONS contradictions; route/page assumptions failing on null/missing fields; rollback-order gaps; merged-but-not-live ambiguity. Same hardened text-only CLI, hard secret filter, and `{zdr:true, allow_fallbacks:false, data_collection:deny}` routing as the GLM red-team. Output the 7-column table: `Finding | Severity P0/P1/P2 | Evidence | Why it matters | Required verification | Suggested action | Gate required?`. Every finding is reconciled by Claude; the auditor authorizes nothing.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1028:**Execution:** post-implementation reviewers operate on a FROZEN diff and are INDEPENDENT — run Codex diff review, GLM-POST red-team, and `glm-audit` (+ optional `m3-audit`) CONCURRENTLY, then reconcile in one pass. Only GLM-PRE is serial (it gates the design). Do not re-freeze and re-fan-out unless a P0/P1 fix changes the diff.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1065:**Two tracks.** FULL packet (A–K) by default. **LITE packet** (D, E, F, G/H if applicable, I) is permitted ONLY when ALL hold and are stated explicitly: re-applies a previously red-teamed pattern; no new or altered invariant; no new schema object; no change to deploy/rollback order; no new auth/RLS/money surface — AND you cite the specific prior GLM finding-set reused by its packet/decision id. GLM-PRE may be skipped on the LITE track ONLY when every condition holds AND **the operator confirms LITE eligibility** (not merely the gate); if any condition is uncertain, run FULL. **GLM-POST and the Human Gate remain mandatory on both tracks — never skipped**, with one recorded exception: `--human-gate-scope irreversible` narrows the STOP (never the review, never the tier) to migrations/SQL, money paths and authz/RLS. Choosing it is a deliberate loosening of this rule, not a default. If GLM-PRE returned clean AND the diff faithfully matches the approved design with no new invariant/interface, the post pass is right-sized to Codex diff review + `glm-audit` at HIGH effort; a second xhigh GLM-POST is required only if the diff introduced logic not in the approved design.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1084:**Evidence depth scales with severity.** P0/P1: full record with cited evidence (and the alternate-path check before any false-positive close). P2: one-line disposition (fix-now / backlog / wontfix) — no deep verification, and P2s never block convergence.
.coverloop/reports/b6a1c56e0655edd4b45d4bde4fcb3a615a2722f6.codex.log:1163:A task is CONVERGED and review STOPS when ALL hold: (1) tests/typecheck/lint/build green; (2) zero unresolved P0/P1; (3) every open finding classified per Section 6; (4) the latest reviewer pass produced no NEW P0/P1. Once converged, remaining P2s are logged as follow-ups, NOT fixed-and-re-reviewed in this task. Re-review after a fix is RE-SCOPED to the changed lines only — reviewers may not raise net-new findings OUTSIDE the fix diff (those go to backlog), but a P0/P1 introduced BY the fix itself must be fixed before convergence. Round budget: max 2 GLM rounds and 2 Codex rounds per task; a 3rd round requires the human operator to explicitly authorize the spend. **Repeated REAL findings past the cap = a SIZE signal, not just a spend decision (v2.5):** if each round keeps surfacing genuine (non-cosmetic) P0/P1s, the change is too large to review well — SPLIT the PR into smaller reviewable units rather than grinding a 4th/5th round on one oversized diff (a single PR that needed 5 Codex rounds is the tell). **The round cap NEVER converts an unresolved P0/P1 into a pass — if the cap is reached with any open P0/P1, that is a STOP escalated to the human operator.** `glm-audit`/`m3-audit` are consistency checks, not part of this nitpick loop.
